Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.839 --> 00:00:03.510 align:start position:0%
 
as the cdc loosens  social distancing guidelines for schools  and most districts reopen classrooms  a new survey finds 59 percent of  students now have the option of  in-person learning for our series school  matters we visited parsippany hills high  school  in new jersey to follow up with a group  of high school educators  we first met eight months ago pedro  hernandez teaches social studies there  sheena graham is a music and chorus  instructor in bridgeport connecticut  emily krieger teaches health in new york  state and matt baker teaches math  in new york city all four are partially  or fully vaccinated they are currently  teaching students  on hybrid schedules and have more  students coming back in person  after spring break how are you all doing  everybody laughs emily stark  emily i'm okay i think i'm better now  than i was if you asked me probably  four or five months ago man  just tired i'm just like epically  permanently tired at this point  sheena i'm glad to be here yeah  i um this has been a journey it's  it's a little overwhelming still yeah  just dealing with  going from being heroes to villains in a  short period of time  pedro how are you doing i'd say i i  adjusted  but i'll tell my kids like sometimes  it'll come out  i hate virtual teaching or at least just  you know remote teaching for the kids  that i have at home  that i just want everyone back in the  classroom because it's just  just a better experience for me and the  kids speaking of  the kids how would you say your kids are  doing  one student said to me are you as afraid  to be around me as i am to be around you  really they take in a lot and if you've  really built relationships with students  this is a stressful time for them being  near you  when they care about you for me i've  really focused on  like shayna said the relationship  building which i do  but i really spend a lot more time in  investing in that because they need  somebody and they need somebody to lean  on  i think the kids have had a year of  trauma that they've been working through  and i think we're trying as best as  we can to address their trauma and help  them  process it from a distance when but when  the teachers are going through trauma  too  also like we just don't have the  resources like  i still don't know what some of my  students look like i'm curious how you  feel  that you've been treated as teachers  through this whole period of time i'll  tell people i'm a teacher we know where  i go in conversation and  they applaud me they thank me and you  know i'm like  thank you but it doesn't really set him  because then there's the other side  where  you're the reason my kid's not back in  school we are working  our butts off every single day to make  sure kids are learning and kids are  working really hard  and then you hear these stories about  kids aren't learning anything and  schools are closed even though  it doesn't feel like we're closed  because i'm working all the time  and that's just it's been really  demoralizing  sometimes how much do you think this has  cost  the kids you teach academically  content wise it's hard um i'm  probably two or three weeks behind where  i was last year two or three weeks  behind yeah they're able to connect  things and i think that's  really one of the biggest parts of  history is being able to explain  and link these things together but even  then  all i'm seeing is ceiling and i have to  see all right uh whose lights are that  all right that's uh  you're seeing ceiling yes the cameras  are on but  i see a lot of ceilings or tapestry or  artwork behind  but i know who the kids are and if i  talk to them they'll respond  but it's just how much are they really  engaged do you think  standardized tests should should not be  given this year yes  yeah my takeaway from last year was we  went through three quarters of a year  normal shut down and we said you know  what  let's make everybody exempt let's not  give a regents exam  yeah and now you've asked us to reinvent  what we're doing in a classroom  asking students to handle remote in  person  and now we're saying hey and now we want  you to take a regents exam  i just don't see why are we doing them  to me it's  it's money those companies want to get  paid to you know distribute them  but you're not thinking about the  student right now the regents exams are  just going to show us what we already  know that this  pandemic has hit different communities  very differently  and i don't trust that they're going to  use that information to like make things  better  so much as make those kids lives harder  when we get back  not to mention taking the test when you  don't know half the stuff on the test is  traumatic for kids like yeah  all of us have memories of sitting down  for a test we didn't study for and  thinking that up please but like so true  but like it's it's going to be just  adding  unnecessary trauma to these kids who  know they're behind we know they're  behind  but here sit and take a three-hour exam  that shows you just how behind you are  yeah and you're setting kids up pretty  much to fail right now  as you look to the fall and the  likelihood that  everyone will be back in school how do  you feel  i think the concern would be  making sure that with the funding that  comes  the mental health people are going to be  there full  force and not just for the kids  for you too for us too i think that  and i i don't want to speak for anybody  here but i know  a lot of teachers who are were broken  with all the stress that this year has  brought  did any of you consider giving up  teaching  yes you did i did so there were days  where i thought you know  i'm not sure i can hold on you know you  find yourself  if i'm honest you cry a lot in the car  so how did you get through it and keep  doing it  this is embarrassing but i'm going to  tell you if you've ever seen the show  the office  [Music]  one of the characters dwight would go  out on sales calls  and he would sit in the car and head  bang  this was my first time in my life  becoming a headbanger i mean i  crank that music up and i'm just out  there going for quite a while  and it's like just i mean it sounds so  silly  but it's hard to do that and then get  out of the car and cry  and so it's like little little things  and so when you get those moments where  the kid  speaks from the heart and just says  you're making a difference that's what  helps you to hold on  and so i know my time's not up  thank you sheena graham for your honesty  and for to matt and to pedro and emily  to all of them for talking to us again  and and and you know how much they've  been through i mean i want to point out  that matt was talking to us  uh a day after the one year anniversary  that one of his fellow  teachers died of covet so he experienced  this very early on  and they've been through so much and  they've been so resilient  the residue is still you can feel it in  the room with them  did you tell all your teachers to wear  bright blue no i don't know what it's a  great picture  you're good at tv we'll be right back  you're watching cbs this morning

